# Blood Relation Reasoning — Part 1 of 4

[info title="Multi-part lesson"]
This lesson is split into 4 parts so each stays a manageable length. This is Part 1 — continue to Part 2 (`blood-relation-reasoning-part-2`) when you're done here.
[/info]

> Blood relation reasoning is the ability to understand family relationships from language, build a family graph, and answer questions about who is related to whom.

[important]
The core idea is simple: convert words like **father**, **mother**, **brother**, **uncle**, and **cousin** into a structured family model, then reason step by step.
[/important]

## 1. What Blood Relation Reasoning Means

Blood relation problems ask questions such as:

- Who is the father of X?
- How is A related to B?
- What is the relation between two people?
- If P is Q's mother and Q is R's sister, then what is P to R?

These questions are not solved by guessing. They are solved by building a family structure and tracing links.

[tip]
A strong blood-relation engine should track **generation**, **gender**, **marriage**, **parent-child links**, and **sibling links** separately.
[/tip]

## 2. Core Family Terms

| Term | Meaning |
|---|---|
| father | male parent |
| mother | female parent |
| son | male child |
| daughter | female child |
| brother | male sibling |
| sister | female sibling |
| husband | male spouse |
| wife | female spouse |
| grandfather | father of a parent |
| grandmother | mother of a parent |
| uncle | brother of a parent |
| aunt | sister of a parent |
| nephew | son of a sibling |
| niece | daughter of a sibling |
| cousin | child of an uncle or aunt |

## 3. The Basic Family Graph

@diagram tree id="basic-family-tree" layout="hierarchical" aria="Basic family tree with grandparents, father, mother, and their children"
node gp [label="Grandparents", shape="pill"]
node father [label="Father"]
node mother [label="Mother"]
node sona [label="Son A"]
node daughterb [label="Daughter B"]
node sonc [label="Son C"]
node daughterd [label="Daughter D"]

edge gp -> father
edge gp -> mother
edge father -> sona
edge father -> daughterb
edge mother -> sonc
edge mother -> daughterd
@/diagram

[info]
A family tree is a graph. Each node is a person. Each edge is a relationship.
[/info]

## 4. Relation Types

Blood relation reasoning uses different kinds of links.

### 4.1 Parent-child link

```text
Parent → Child
```

### 4.2 Sibling link

```text
Sibling ↔ Sibling
```

### 4.3 Spouse link

```text
Husband ↔ Wife
```

### 4.4 Generation link

```text
Grandparent → Parent → Child → Grandchild
```

## 5. Important Rule: Gender and Relation Are Different

A person's role and gender are not the same thing.

For example:

- "parent" does not tell gender
- "child" does not tell gender
- "sibling" does not tell gender

So the engine should store them separately. A typical pipeline: **Sentence → Extract entities → Identify relations → Build family graph → Propagate constraints → Answer.**

## 6. Family Graph Representation

A good internal model looks like this:

```text
Person {
  name
  gender
  parents[]
  children[]
  spouse
  siblings[]
}
```

For example:

```text
A = male
B = female
A spouse = B
A children = [C, D]
B children = [C, D]
```

This makes reasoning much easier.

## 7. Generation Rules

Generation is the simplest way to understand family relations.

- One step up: parent
- Two steps up: grandparent
- One step down: child
- Two steps down: grandchild

### Example chain

@diagram tree id="generation-chain" layout="hierarchical" aria="Generation chain showing two steps up and two steps down from a person"
node x [label="Person X"]
node gcparent [label="Parent"]
node gparent [label="Grandparent"]
node gcchild [label="Child"]
node gchild [label="Grandchild"]

edge x -> gcparent
edge gcparent -> gparent
edge x -> gcchild
edge gcchild -> gchild
@/diagram

## 8. Sibling Rules

Two people are siblings if they share at least one parent.

Example:

- A and B are siblings if both have the same father or same mother.

[warning]
Half-siblings may share only one parent. A careful engine should not assume every sibling relation means both parents are identical.
[/warning]

## 9. Uncle and Aunt

An uncle is the brother of a parent.

An aunt is the sister of a parent.

@diagram tree id="uncle-aunt" layout="hierarchical" aria="Uncle and aunt relations branching from a grandparent"
node uagp [label="Grandparent"]
node uaparent [label="Parent"]
node uachild [label="Child"]
node uncle [label="Uncle"]
node cousin1 [label="Cousin"]
node aunt [label="Aunt"]
node cousin2 [label="Cousin"]

edge uagp -> uaparent
edge uagp -> uncle
edge uagp -> aunt
edge uaparent -> uachild
edge uncle -> cousin1
edge aunt -> cousin2
@/diagram

## 10. Cousin Relation

Cousins are children of siblings.

If A is the sibling of B, then:

- A's child and B's child are cousins.

```text
Sibling 1 → Child 1
Sibling 2 → Child 2
Child 1 ↔ Child 2 = cousins
```

## 11. Direct and Indirect Relations

Some relations are direct:

- father
- mother
- son
- daughter
- husband
- wife

Some are indirect:

- grandfather
- grandmother
- uncle
- aunt
- cousin
- nephew
- niece

Indirect relations are usually derived from direct links.

## 12. Example 1

> “A is B's father. B is C's sister. How is A related to C?”

### Step 1: Parse

```text
A is father of B
B is sister of C
```

### Step 2: Build graph

**A → B → C**

### Step 3: Reason

- A is parent of B
- B and C are siblings
- Therefore A is also parent of C

### Answer

[important]
A is **father** of C.
[/important]

## 13. Example 2

> “P is Q's mother. Q is R's brother. What is P to R?”

### Reasoning

- P is mother of Q
- Q and R are brothers, so they are siblings
- If P is mother of Q, then P is mother of R as well

### Answer

[important]
P is **mother** of R.
[/important]

## 14. Example 3

> “M is the sister of N. N is the father of O. How is M related to O?”

### Reasoning

- M is sibling of N
- N is father of O
- M is sibling of O's father
- So M is O's aunt

### Answer

[important]
M is **aunt** of O.
[/important]
